In this paper we give a closed formula for the graded dimension of the cyclotomic quiver Hecke algebra $R^\Lambda( eta)$ associated to an {\it arbitrary} symmetrizable Cartan matrix $A=(a_{ij})_{i,j}\in I$, where $\Lambda\in P^+$ and $ eta\in Q_n^+$. As applications, we obtain some {\it necessary and sufficient conditions} for the KLR idempotent $e(
u)$ (for any $
u\in I^ eta$) to be nonzero in the cyclotomic quiver Hecke algebra $R^\Lambda( eta)$. We prove several level reduction results which decomposes $\dim R^\Lambda( eta)$ into a sum of some products of $\dim R^{\Lambda^i}( eta_i)$ with $\Lambda=\sum_i\Lambda^i$ and $ eta=\sum_{i} eta_i$, where $\Lambda^i\in P^+,  eta^i\in Q^+$ for each $i$. We construct some explicit monomial bases for the subspaces $e(\widetilde{
u})R^\Lambda( eta)e(\mu)$ and $e(\widetilde{
u})R^\Lambda( eta)e(\mu)$ of $R^\Lambda( eta)$, where $\mu\in I^ eta$ is {\it arbitrary} and $\widetilde{
u}\in I^ eta$ is a certain specific $n$-tuple (see Section 4).Finally, we use our graded dimension formulae to provide some examples which show that $R^\Lambda(n)$ is in general not graded free over its natural embedded subalgebra $R^\Lambda(m)$ with $m<n$.

In the present work, we study the two-dimensional anisotropic KPZ equation (AKPZ), which is formally given by  egin{equation*} \partial_t h=	frac12 \Delta h + \lambda ((\partial_1 h)^2)-(\partial_2 h)^2) +\xi\,, \end{equation*} where $\xi$ denotes a space-time white noise and $\lambda>0$ is the so-called coupling constant. The AKPZ equation is a {\it critical} SPDE, meaning that not only it is analytically ill-posed but also the breakthrough path-wise techniques for singular SPDEs [M. Hairer, Ann. Math. 2014] and [M. Gubinelli, P. Imkeller and N. Perkowski, Forum of Math., Pi, 2015] are not applicable. As shown in [G. Cannizzaro, D. Erhard, F. Toninelli, arXiv, 2020], the equation regularised at scale $N$ has a diffusion coefficient that diverges logarithmically as the regularisation is removed in the limit $N	o\infty$. Here, we study the \emph{weak coupling limit} where $\lambda=\lambda_N=\hat\lambda/\sqrt{\log N}$: this is the correct scaling that guarantees that the nonlinearity has a still non-trivial but non-divergent effect. In fact, as $N	o\infty$ the sequence of equations converges to the linear stochastic heat equation  egin{equation*} \partial_t h =	frac{
u_{m eff}}{2} \Delta h + \sqrt{
u_{m eff}}\xi\,, \end{equation*} where $
u_{m eff} >1$ is explicit and depends non-trivially on $\hat\lambda$. This is the first full renormalization-type result for a critical, singular SPDE which cannot be linearised via Cole-Hopf or any other transformation.

